1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,520
In May 1970, Los Angeles radio station KPFK received an anonymous phone call, leading them to a cassette tape hidden in a public phone booth. It begins like this.

2
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:26,760
Hello, this is Bernadine Dorn. I'm going to read a declaration of a state of war. This is the first communication from the weatherman underground.

3
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:41,100
Bernadine Dorn is my mother. She's recording this tape when she's just 28 years old, surrounded by a few friends in a safe house in San Francisco, a one-room apartment they've rented using a fake ID.

4
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:52,240
The place is crowded, and most of the people in the room are even younger than she is, student activists and grad school dropouts in their early to mid-20s.

5
00:00:52,240 --> 00:01:01,020
There's a device the size of a lunchbox set up in the middle of a table, an old-school tape cassette player with a red record button.

6
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:12,380
All over the world, people fighting American imperialism look to America's youth to use our strategic position behind enemy lines to join forces in the destruction of the empire.

7
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:17,920
Kids know the lines are drawn. Revolution is touching all of our lives.

8
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:25,120
They've written this statement together, over a bunch of sleepless nights on a stolen typewriter.

9
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,600
Revisions marked in pen and retyped over and over to get it right.

10
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:33,860
It's a collaborative effort, a group project.

11
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:41,940
But they all understand, as the leader of the organization, the public face, it would be Bernadine delivering their message.

12
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:45,220
Freaks are revolutionaries, and revolutionaries are freaks.

13
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:48,060
If you want to find us, this is where we are.

14
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:57,640
In every tribe, commune, dormitory, farmhouse, barracks, and townhouse, where kids are making love, smoking dope, and loading guns.

15
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:00,620
Fugitives from American justice are free to go.

16
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,360
It's funny for me to listen to this tape now, 50 years later.

17
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:09,199
It's not just how 1970 it is.

18
00:02:09,699 --> 00:02:13,000
Tribe and commune, making love, smoking dope.

19
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:15,520
It's also how young she sounds.

20
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:18,240
Her voice is a bit shaky.

21
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:24,320
Despite the fact she later became famous, infamous, as a symbol of revolutionary rage,

22
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:27,180
my mother has always been a private person.

23
00:02:28,079 --> 00:02:28,600
Reserved.

24
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:29,940
Kind of shy.

25
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:34,380
So I can hear her forcing herself to say these words.

26
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,240
Driving herself to do something that doesn't come naturally to her.

27
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:41,560
Because she believes somebody has to do something.

28
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,120
She has to do something.

29
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:49,820
The parents of privileged kids have been saying for years that the revolution was a game for us.

30
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:54,600
Tens of thousands have learned that protests and marches don't do it.

31
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,520
Revolutionary violence is the only way.

32
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:02,160
My mom was actually one of those privileged kids herself.

33
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:06,760
She'd recently graduated from the University of Chicago Law School,

34
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:10,600
one of the most prestigious and conservative law schools in the country.

35
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,580
John Ashcroft, George W. Bush's attorney general, was one of her classmates.

36
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:19,500
But she'd grown up in modest circumstances.

37
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:21,340
Lower middle class.

38
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:23,500
The granddaughter of four immigrants.

39
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:25,340
A straight-A student.

40
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:26,300
A cheerleader.

41
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:29,200
A kid who tap-danced at the American Legion.

42
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:31,600
Within the next 14 days,

43
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:35,160
we will attack a symbol or institution of American injustice.

44
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:40,940
There are very few positive things to say about planting an explosive in a government building.

45
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:43,340
But as an attention-getting device,

46
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:47,460
it's hard to argue with the effectiveness of that bomb that went off here at the State Department.

47
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:49,160
The bomb exploded early this morning.

48
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:53,560
Credit for the Capitol bombing was claimed in a letter received by the Associated Press today,

49
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:55,460
signed by the Weathermen.

50
00:03:55,460 --> 00:04:01,260
The Weathermen promises more attacks on the establishment around the entire country starting next week.

51
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:07,299
Bernadine's declaration of a state of war set off a campaign of anti-government violence.

52
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:11,399
It would turn her and her friends into outlaws and symbols.

53
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:18,320
Her trademark miniskirt and knee-high boots, straight brown hair and sunglasses would wind up on wanted posters,

54
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:21,860
newscasts, and underground newspapers across the country.

55
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:26,540
And she would spend the next decade and more on the run from the FBI.

56
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:33,200
Angela Davis was replaced on the FBI's 10 Most Wanted list this afternoon by Bernadine Ray Dorn,

57
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:35,680
described as an underground leader of the Weathermen.

58
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:40,980
The FBI says she advocates bombings, violent revolution, and terrorist attacks.

59
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:47,220
J. Edgar Hoover, the head of the Bureau, called Bernadine the most dangerous woman in America.

60
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:53,140
But those sound bites don't really capture her, as a person or as a symbol.

61
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:57,479
They don't capture what drives her, and people like her.

62
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:01,020
Which is important because she was part of a moment,

63
00:05:01,539 --> 00:05:05,140
not unique in American history, but remarkably rare,

64
00:05:05,599 --> 00:05:09,380
when middle-class white kids took up arms against their own country.

65
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:13,860
A country they realized that was killing Vietnamese people abroad,

66
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,360
and black people here at home.

67
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:20,580
These kids abandoned their promising futures,

68
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,700
severed ties with family,

69
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:25,659
some, including my mom, would go to jail.

70
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:27,940
Others were killed in the struggle.

71
00:05:28,659 --> 00:05:32,719
But they were all convinced their privilege put them on the wrong side of history.

72
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,300
To get on the right side,

73
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:38,700
they were willing to blow up the world they were supposed to inherit.

74
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:50,440
My name is Zayd Ayersdorn,

75
00:05:50,659 --> 00:05:52,060
and this is a family history,

76
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:53,860
because I was born underground.

77
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:55,880
For the first years of my life,

78
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:58,200
my parents and I were on the run from the FBI.

79
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:01,360
When people ask me about my childhood today,

80
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,780
I still find it kind of hard to explain.

81
00:06:04,659 --> 00:06:05,960
Some parts are simple.

82
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:08,060
We used fake names.

83
00:06:08,739 --> 00:06:09,460
Outside the house,

84
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:10,140
my parents,

85
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:11,780
Bernadine Dorn and Bill Ayers,

86
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:13,640
went by Rose and Tony.

87
00:06:14,359 --> 00:06:15,560
I was just called Z.

88
00:06:16,419 --> 00:06:18,200
We paid for everything in cash,

89
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:19,919
made calls from payphones.

90
00:06:20,479 --> 00:06:22,320
My parents didn't apply for jobs

91
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:23,880
that required background checks

92
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:25,359
or social security numbers.

93
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:27,820
But our day-to-day life

94
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:29,460
wasn't that out of the ordinary.

95
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,120
We got up in the mornings,

96
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:32,720
went to work or school.

97
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:34,580
We lived in a one-room apartment

98
00:06:34,580 --> 00:06:36,440
in a fifth-floor walk-up in Harlem.

99
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:38,180
Literally one room,

100
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:40,020
the kitchenette on one side

101
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:42,380
and a curtained-off bath area on the other.

102
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:45,080
My mom worked as a waitress

103
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:46,120
near Lincoln Center.

104
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:48,220
My dad baked in the bread shop

105
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:49,420
on 125th Street.

106
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:50,800
It felt,

107
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,420
and I guess this is probably true for most kids,

108
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:54,880
no matter what their circumstances.

109
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:56,240
But to me,

110
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:58,020
it all just felt normal.

111
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:01,440
I knew from my very first memories

112
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:03,300
when I was two or three years old

113
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:05,440
that the FBI was chasing us.

114
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,740
But I didn't know exactly what FBI was,

115
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:10,580
why they, or it,

116
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:11,900
wanted to catch us,

117
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,840
or what would happen if they did.

118
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,600
It felt more abstract,

119
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:18,580
a childhood boogeyman,

120
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:20,380
something I knew was bad

121
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:21,700
the same way I knew,

122
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:23,380
like any kid knows,

123
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:25,520
that my family had to be good.

124
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:27,700
Did you know that you had secrets

125
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:29,500
that you couldn't talk about?

126
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:30,640
I don't know what you knew,

127
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:33,100
but we tried to make it fun for you.

128
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,400
They explained the underground to me

129
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,280
in terms a kid could understand.

130
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:39,820
We were fighting an unjust empire,

131
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:41,460
like Luke Skywalker,

132
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:42,740
or Robin Hood,

133
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:44,340
stealing from the rich

134
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:45,460
and giving to the poor.

135
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:47,540
I actually dressed up as Robin

136
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:49,200
for my first few Halloweens.

137
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,820
So, like most people,

138
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:54,380
I grew up thinking my parents were heroes.

139
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:56,680
But as I got older,

140
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,700
of course, it got more complicated.

141
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:00,219
You grow up,

142
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:02,280
you start to see some of your parents' flaws,

143
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:03,840
their contradictions.

144
00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:05,380
You realize,

145
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:06,860
maybe you don't know everything

146
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:07,940
you thought you knew,

147
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:11,000
that maybe they kept things from you.

148
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,860
What's it like having me do

149
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:13,820
this kind of research

150
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:15,280
into your past?

151
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:17,920
I, you know,

152
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:19,160
it's wonderful

153
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:21,460
that you're interested even,

154
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:22,800
really.

155
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:24,380
But,

156
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:27,760
it scares me.

157
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:29,120
What does that mean?

158
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:30,280
Well, if I think,

159
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:30,660
you know,

160
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:32,100
by you doing this project,

161
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:33,159
you've made me think,

162
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:34,659
you know,

163
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:35,600
are there secrets?

164
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:39,240
What to keep is a secret

165
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:40,860
and what not to keep.

166
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:47,260
My mom is the most idealistic person I know.

167
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:50,220
And both definitions of the word apply here.

168
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:52,800
She's characterized by idealism

169
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:55,720
and unrealistically aiming for perfection.

170
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:57,880
She sees everything

171
00:08:57,880 --> 00:08:59,420
through the lens of the struggle

172
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:00,460
against injustice.

173
00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:02,180
And I mean everything.

174
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:04,460
One example.

175
00:09:04,460 --> 00:09:05,840
I recently found

176
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:07,000
a present she gave me

177
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:07,760
when I was a kid,

178
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:09,260
when she was locked up

179
00:09:09,260 --> 00:09:10,300
in Manhattan's

180
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:11,800
Metropolitan Correctional Center

181
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:13,780
after 11 years on the run.

182
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:16,660
It's a calendar she made me

183
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:17,320
in her cell,

184
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:19,660
out of construction paper and yarn,

185
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:21,780
tracing boxes for the days,

186
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:23,520
filling in notable dates,

187
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:24,840
illustrating the months

188
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:26,460
with line drawings and photos.

189
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:29,080
It's a nice present,

190
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:31,200
thoughtful and handmade,

191
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:32,820
something I could put up

192
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:33,460
over my bed

193
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:34,800
to remind me of her

194
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:35,860
while she was in jail.

195
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:37,480
I found it comforting.

196
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:40,380
But when I dug it up recently,

197
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:41,740
I noticed something

198
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:43,540
about the holidays she's marked.

199
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:46,440
December 25th is empty.

200
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:47,880
There's no Christmas

201
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:49,260
or Christmas Eve.

202
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:50,900
No Hanukkah either.

203
00:09:51,420 --> 00:09:53,380
But there is December 2nd,

204
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:55,040
John Brown hanged.

205
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:56,660
December 4th,

206
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:58,060
Fred Hampton murdered.

207
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:00,080
December 15th,

208
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:01,760
Sitting Bull assassinated.

209
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:03,500
And the other months

210
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:04,440
are like that too.

211
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:06,640
April doesn't have my birthday,

212
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:07,860
the 26th,

213
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:08,940
but it does have

214
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:09,560
the assassination

215
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:10,860
of Martin Luther King,

216
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:13,160
the killing of little Bobby Hutton,

217
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:16,220
the Colfax Massacre of 1873.

218
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:18,980
All these anniversaries,

219
00:10:19,220 --> 00:10:21,100
commemorating people who resisted.

220
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:23,800
American revolutionary martyrs

221
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,140
on the day they were executed,

222
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:26,220
lynched,

223
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:27,500
assassinated.

224
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:29,300
Keep in mind,

225
00:10:29,460 --> 00:10:30,700
I was five years old

226
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:31,360
at the time.

227
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:35,060
So that's who she is,

228
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:36,180
who she's been

229
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:37,640
for as long as I can remember.

230
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:39,520
She's an idealist,

231
00:10:40,020 --> 00:10:41,420
someone who sees clearly,

232
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:42,700
zealously,

233
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:43,900
the difference

234
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:45,000
between right and wrong,

235
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:47,140
who believes in sacrifice

236
00:10:47,140 --> 00:10:47,980
for the struggle,

237
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:50,060
and expects everyone else,

238
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:51,340
including her kids,

239
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:52,860
to believe in it too.

240
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:56,380
And I don't think quite like her

241
00:10:56,380 --> 00:10:58,560
in those moral absolutes.

242
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:00,400
It's why I became a writer

243
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:01,780
instead of an activist.

244
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:03,500
Because I'm interested

245
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:04,520
in the messiness

246
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:05,840
of what drives people,

247
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:07,340
not just politically,

248
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:08,320
but personally.

249
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:10,160
Because I've seen

250
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:11,420
the costs of the struggle

251
00:11:11,420 --> 00:11:12,200
up close.

252
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:13,820
My brothers and I

253
00:11:13,820 --> 00:11:15,380
had to live with the consequences

254
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:16,620
of our parents' actions.

255
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:19,100
And I grew up wondering,

256
00:11:19,580 --> 00:11:20,660
how could my mom and dad

257
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:21,880
choose to have children

258
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:22,980
if they were willing

259
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:24,220
to take those risks?

260
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:27,600
But I've also been thinking lately

261
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:29,300
about what it meant to them

262
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:30,600
to resist.

263
00:11:31,439 --> 00:11:32,920
To be willing to fight back

264
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:33,580
violently

265
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:35,040
against a racist

266
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:36,340
and unjust system.

267
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:38,680
Because these past few years

268
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:39,320
have shown us,

269
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:40,320
even if we weren't

270
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:41,320
paying attention before,

271
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:42,840
that white supremacy

272
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:44,220
is alive and well

273
